// Section: Ratings & feedback.
{
  "$schema": "../../../docs/rules.fragment.schema.json",
  "screens": [
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.delivery_feedback",
      "priority": 57,
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "hasTextContaining": "How did this delivery go"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.safety_feedback",
      "priority": 58,
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "any": [
            {
              "hasTextContaining": "Feedback about your safety"
            },
            {
              "hasTextContaining": "How safe did you feel on this delivery"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "2026-07-30 corpus pass — the 0.230.0 REDESIGNED hub ('Overall rating <N>' + the Silver/Gold/Platinum gem ladder + a 'Your rating factors' points list) never renders the literal word 'ratings' anywhere on the screen. The original conjunct only matched it by ACCIDENT, off the side-nav drawer's 'Ratings' menu row: the two fielded drawer-open frames (07-29 15:15:49 / 15:17:01) classified `ratings`, while the two drawer-closed frames of the SAME screen (…UNKNOWN__3544f5 / …UNKNOWN__dd3695) fell to UNKNOWN. Recognition must not depend on an unrelated overlay being open, so a second arm anchors on the redesign's own copy. 'Your rating factors' is corpus-unique (0 other snapshots) and the arm still demands both rate labels, so it cannot claim the single-metric drill-downs (performance_rate_detail / customer_rating_detail render ONE metric and no 'Your rating factors' header). PRIVACY: no customer PII — the dasher's own aggregate rating counters and fixed program copy. #962 CLOSED the parse residual this comment used to document: every field below is now a `coalesce` of the LEGACY `textView_title`-sibling anchor FIRST and a redesign anchor SECOND. Legacy-first is deliberate and load-bearing — on an old-layout frame the legacy branch is non-null, so `coalesce` returns it verbatim and the committed old-layout goldens stay byte-identical; the redesign branch can only ever be reached where the legacy one reads null. The redesign layout is fully ID-LESS (Compose): a factor row renders `label → value → 'N of M points' → band` as bare sibling TextViews, so the redesign branches anchor on the exact factor LABEL text and read `sibling(1)`. That label is app chrome (stable vocabulary), the value is its immediate next sibling in every fielded row incl. 'Customer rating' (whose row carries extra icon/points siblings only AFTER the value), and the anchor is byte-identical in shape to the legacy one — the only thing that changed is which node carries the label. OPPORTUNISTIC CAPTURE — DISPLAY/RECORDS ONLY (dev ruling 2026-07-30): everything this rule parses updates ONLY when the dasher happens to view the Ratings surface, so it may be null or arbitrarily stale forever. No decision path — evaluator, state machine, effects, analytics folds — may consume these fields; protect-stats in particular is a BEHAVIOR, not a calculation, and needs no rating input. Enforced by `RatingsSnapshotIsDisplayOnlyTest` (:core:state) and stated authoritatively on the `RatingsSnapshot` KDoc. That is also why this rule declares no `state.flow` and no effects: a ratings frame must never move the machine.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.ratings",
      "priority": 90,
      "bind": {
        "ratingsHubContent": {
          "comment": "#962 — the parse SCOPE for `tierLabel`, and the one anchor on this rule that must not be a text shape. DoorDash renders the dasher's reward tier TWICE on a drawer-open frame: once as the hub header chip (the redesign's own 'Silver' above 'Overall rating 73') and once in the SIDE-NAV drawer ('[icon] Silver' on 0.230.0, and a bare 'Silver' TextView on the pre-redesign builds — corpus frame ratings/2026-02-14_20-42-32__RATINGS_VIEW__86bd7ffc.json has exactly that). A whole-tree text find would therefore read the tier off an unrelated overlay — the same accidental-overlay dependency the require block above was fixed to stop depending on. `side_nav_content_container` is the container holding the HUB CONTENT and NOT the drawer (the drawer lives in its sibling `side_nav_container`), it is present on BOTH the legacy and the redesign layouts, and it is not a target bind name, so it enumerates no consent capability. `optional: true` — a bind miss must never disqualify the rule. RESIDUAL: a bind miss falls the parse back to the whole tree (compiler semantics, `bindings[name] ?: tree`); DFS pre-order still visits the content container before the drawer, so the hub's own chip still wins where one is rendered.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "side_nav_content_container"
          },
          "optional": true
        }
      },
      "require": {
        "any": [
          {
            "allTextContainsAll": [
              "ratings",
              "acceptance rate",
              "completion rate",
              "overall"
            ]
          },
          {
            "allTextContainsAll": [
              "your rating factors",
              "acceptance rate",
              "completion rate"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "parse": {
        "as": "ratings",
        "fields": {
          // #962 — the points-based redesign (DoorDash 0.230.0). Every re-anchored
          // field below is `coalesce: [ <legacy textView_title sibling>, <redesign
          // label sibling> ]`. LEGACY FIRST is load-bearing: on an old-layout frame
          // the legacy branch is non-null so `coalesce` returns it unchanged and the
          // committed old-layout goldens do not move; the redesign branch is only
          // ever reached where the legacy anchor reads null.
          "acceptanceRate": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Acceptance"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              },
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "hasText": "Acceptance rate"
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              }
            ]
          },
          "completionRate": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Completion"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              },
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "hasText": "Completion rate"
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              }
            ]
          },
          "onTimeRate": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "On-time"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              },
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "hasText": "On-time rate"
                },
                "transform": "parsePercent"
              }
            ]
          },
          // The redesign's 'Customer rating' row renders label → value → a
          // 'Rating value icon' View → 'N of N points' → band, so the star value
          // is still `sibling(1)`; the extra siblings all sit AFTER it.
          "customerRating": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Customer rating"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "transform": "toDouble"
              },
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "hasText": "Customer rating"
                },
                "transform": "toDouble"
              }
            ]
          },
          // The redesign renamed this factor 'Last 30-day orders' (it is now a
          // scored rating factor, not a bare counter). The legacy branch's
          // 'Deliveries (last 30 days)' anchor does NOT match the legacy corpus
          // either — the fielded legacy label is 'Deliveries last 30 days', with no
          // parentheses — so that field reads null on every committed legacy frame.
          // Left as-is deliberately: correcting it would CHANGE a value on a legacy
          // golden entry, which is out of scope for this re-anchor (#962 follow-up).
          "deliveriesLast30Days": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Deliveries (last 30 days)"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "transform": "toInt"
              },
              {
                "siblingOf": {
                  "hasText": "Last 30-day orders"
                },
                "transform": "toInt"
              }
            ]
          },
          // #962 NEW — 'Quality rate' is a rating factor the pre-redesign hub did
          // not have at all, so there is no legacy branch to coalesce with.
          "qualityRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "hasText": "Quality rate"
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          // #962 NEW — the headline points score, rendered as ONE node
          // ('Overall rating 73'), so it is a find + regex extraction rather than a
          // label/value sibling pair. The `^…$` anchoring keeps it off the legacy
          // hub's bare 'Overall' tab label and off the 'Your overall rating is 74'
          // explainer sheet (a different screen).
          "overallRatingPoints": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^Overall rating \\d+$"
            },
            "transform": {
              "regex": {
                "pattern": "(\\d+)",
                "group": 1,
                "then": "toInt"
              }
            }
          },
          // #962 NEW — the raw reward-tier label, recorded as a FACT only (dev
          // ruling 2026-07-30: no per-factor points, no band labels, no tier
          // gamification — the tier ladder is DoorDash's program vocabulary and is
          // deliberately not modelled). Scoped to `$ratingsHubContent` so it can
          // never be read off the side-nav drawer's own tier chip; see the bind
          // comment above. The enumeration is rule DATA (DoorDash tier names in the
          // DoorDash ruleset), and an unlisted/absent tier fails NULL rather than
          // guessing.
          "tierLabel": {
            "from": "$ratingsHubContent",
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^(?:Silver|Gold|Platinum)$"
            }
          },
          "lifetimeDeliveries": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Lifetime deliveries"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "toInt"
          },
          "originalItemsFoundRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Original items found"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          "totalItemsFoundRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Total items found"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          "substitutionIssuesRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Substitution issues"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          "itemsWithQualityIssuesRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Items with quality issues"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          "itemsWrongOrMissingRate": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Items wrong or missing"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "parsePercent"
          },
          "lifetimeShoppingOrders": {
            "siblingOf": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "textView_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Lifetime shopping orders"
                }
              ]
            },
            "transform": "toInt"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.safety",
      "priority": 91,
      "require": {
        "allTextContainsAll": [
          "safedash",
          "safety tools",
          "report safety issue",
          "share your location"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#796 — the post-task 'How was this task?' feedback prompt (a thumbs-up/thumbs-down header, either bare or expanded into the issue-category picker: 'App issues'/'Navigation issues'/'Pick up issues'/'Drop off issues'/'Earnings'/'Safety issues'/'Other' + a free-text field + Submit). RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev decision 2026-07-17, batch #796): no state.flow, no parse. Anchor 'How was this task?' is corpus-unique (0 other snapshots) and covers both the bare and expanded shapes. PRIVACY: no customer PII (fixed feedback-category labels only; the free-text field is empty in the captured frames).",
      "id": "doordash.screen.task_feedback",
      "priority": 59,
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "hasTextContaining": "How was this task"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#796 — the one-time 'Introducing Quality Rate' announcement card ('You can now see your Quality Rate in the app…' + Got it). Part of the Dasher Rewards / Quality Rate program surface. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev decision 2026-07-17, batch #796): no state.flow, no parse. Anchor 'Introducing Quality Rate' is corpus-unique (0 other snapshots). PRIVACY: no PII (program copy only).",
      "id": "doordash.screen.quality_rate_intro",
      "priority": 115,
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "hasTextContaining": "Introducing Quality Rate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#796/#865 — a performance-metric DETAIL screen (Completion rate / On-time rate / Quality rate / Acceptance rate): a metric header + score, a 'Last 100 <unit>' breakdown, a 'View all <unit>' button, and an 'About <metric> rate' explainer. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev decision 2026-07-17, batch #796): no state.flow, no parse — the app already ships doordash.screen.ratings (the combined ratings hub); these are the single-metric drill-downs, which that rule's four-way 'ratings'+'acceptance rate'+'completion rate'+'overall' conjunct legitimately does NOT match. #865 BROADENED the anchor to the two UNIT variants of the SAME screen family: the order-denominated metrics render 'Last 100 orders' + 'View all orders', the offer-denominated Acceptance rate renders 'Last 100 offers' + 'View all offers' (the only textual difference — the layout, the 'About …' explainer and the tips section are identical), so it stays ONE intent rather than a near-duplicate sibling rule. All four anchor strings are corpus-unique (0 other snapshots); each variant requires its OWN header+button PAIR, so the sibling list modal (which shares the header but has NO 'View all …' button) still never collides. PRIVACY: no customer PII — the dasher's own aggregate counters + fixed program copy. 2026-07-30 corpus pass added a THIRD arm for the COUNT metric's drill-down ('Last 30-day orders'), the one rating factor in the 0.230.0 redesign that is a running total rather than a rate. Same family and same layout as the two rate arms (metric header + score + band, 'Points toward your overall rating', the collapsible band table, an 'About <metric>' explainer), but it has NO 'Last 100 …' sample window and therefore no 'View all …' button, so neither existing arm could reach it — 5 fielded frames fell to UNKNOWN on 07-29 (…UNKNOWN__b062f1 / __1aa7db / __c5f37f / __8dfab5 / __b1ac01). Anchored on the header + explainer PAIR: 'About last 30-day orders' is corpus-unique (0 other snapshots) and appears ONLY on the drill-down, so the redesigned ratings HUB — which also renders a 'Last 30-day orders' factor row but no explainer — is never claimed (and the hub is claimed first anyway at priority 90).",
      "id": "doordash.screen.performance_rate_detail",
      "priority": 116,
      "require": {
        "any": [
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Last 100 orders"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "View all orders"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Last 100 offers"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "View all offers"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Last 30-day orders"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "About last 30-day orders"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#796/#865 — the 'Last 100 orders' LIST modal reached from a metric detail's 'View all orders' (a tab row All / Completed / Unassigned / Excluded over a store+date order list, with a Close affordance). RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev decision 2026-07-17, batch #796): no state.flow, no parse. #865 RE-ANCHORED off the tab row: the All/Completed/Unassigned/Excluded chips scroll out of the accessibility tree as soon as the driver scrolls the list, so every scrolled frame fell out of recognition (6 fielded UNKNOWNs). The scroll-stable pair is the pinned modal chrome — the 'Last 100 orders' header + the exact-desc 'Close' affordance — which survives to the extreme scrolled frame that renders NOTHING else (capture …UNKNOWN__4f4914). The metric-detail sibling also renders 'Last 100 orders' but has no 'Close' (it is a pushed screen with a 'Navigate up' toolbar, not a modal), so the two stay apart; and even a hypothetical detail-in-a-modal would be claimed first by performance_rate_detail's lower priority number. PRIVACY: no customer PII — merchant/store names + dates + 'No issues' status only, all driver-owned and kept.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.performance_orders_list",
      "priority": 117,
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "Last 100 orders"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasDesc": "Close"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#796 — the 'Dasher Rewards' program screens ('What are Dasher Rewards?' explainer AND the tier/rewards board — 'Available rewards' / 'How to unlock Silver level' + the reward-row recycler). RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev decision 2026-07-17, batch #796): no state.flow, no parse. Anchored on the viewId `reward_info_epoxy_recyclerview` — corpus-unique (0 other snapshots) and present on BOTH sub-screens, so one rule covers them. This id is deliberately used INSTEAD of the 'Dasher Rewards' text (which also appears in the existing ratings/ hub snapshots, 9 corpus frames) and INSTEAD of `reward_name_text_view` (which also appears in ratings, 4 frames) — the recycler-container id is the clean generic anchor that isolates the standalone program screens. PRIVACY: no customer PII (program copy + the dasher's own aggregate rating figures, which are the dasher's own data).",
      "id": "doordash.screen.dasher_rewards",
      "priority": 118,
      "require": {
        "any": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "reward_info_epoxy_recyclerview"
            }
          },
          {
            // #962 — the 0.230.0 REDESIGN of the same rewards board, reached from
            // the points hub's 'View your rewards'. It is a Compose surface: the
            // whole `reward_info_epoxy_recyclerview` recycler the first arm anchors
            // on is gone, and the only ids left are the app-shell containers
            // (`side_nav_content_container` & co.), so the redesign arm has to be
            // text-anchored. 3 fielded frames on 07-30 (…UNKNOWN__ee13be /
            // __9df628 / __60a35e — the same board with the drawer closed, opening
            // and open). Anchored on the PAIR 'Rewards to unlock' (the locked-tier
            // section header) + 'Keep your overall rating high to unlock more
            // rewards' (the current-tier subtitle); both are corpus-unique (0 other
            // snapshots) and both are tier-INDEPENDENT copy, so the arm does not
            // encode Silver/Gold/Platinum and keeps working as the dasher's tier
            // moves. UNDER-MATCH RESIDUAL: at Platinum there is nothing left to
            // unlock, so 'Rewards to unlock' may not render and that frame would
            // fall to UNKNOWN — the safe direction (#857: never claim from absence).
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Rewards to unlock"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Keep your overall rating high to unlock more rewards"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#962 — the overall-rating EXPLAINER sheet, reached from the redesigned ratings hub's 'info/line' affordance: a 'Your overall rating is <N>' header, the sentence explaining that factor points combine into the overall rating, the Silver/Gold/Platinum gem ladder with its 55/75/85 pts thresholds, an 'Overall rating … <N> / 100 pts' row and a Got it / Learn more footer. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev ruling 2026-07-30, #962): no state.flow, no parse — the hub itself (doordash.screen.ratings) is the parse surface for `overallRatingPoints`/`tierLabel`, and this sheet is a duplicate presentation of the same two facts plus the threshold ladder we deliberately do NOT model. 3 fielded frames on 07-30 (…UNKNOWN__288ce1 / __3b3fe5 / __b1b6de — the sheet at three render stages). The sheet is fully id-less (its only view id is `android:id/content`), so the anchor is a text PAIR: a `^Your overall rating is \\d+$` shape plus the 'raise your reward status' tail of the explainer sentence. Both are corpus-unique (0 other snapshots); the regex is `^…$`-anchored so it cannot be satisfied by the hub's own 'Overall rating <N>' header (different wording) and the second conjunct keeps it off any other sheet that might quote a score. PRIVACY: no customer PII — the dasher's own score plus fixed program copy.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.overall_rating_explainer",
      "priority": 137,
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^Your overall rating is \\d+$"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "raise your reward status"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#962 — the PER-ORDER feedback drill-down opened from a quality/completion metric's order list: a store + date header ('<store> · Jul 28 • No issues') over the four per-order check rows the platform scored — 'Customer received order' / 'Correct order delivered' / 'No missing items' / 'No damaged items' — each optionally expanded with its own 'No issues reported …' detail line, plus a Close affordance. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev ruling 2026-07-30, #962): no state.flow, no parse — it is a retrospective per-order receipt, not a live lifecycle surface, and the aggregate `qualityRate` is already parsed off the hub. 10 fielded frames on 07-30 (…UNKNOWN__7e986d / __e472de / __374a1d / __eef9b3 / __5cc8fe / __da93d3 / __473b9c / __03fc09 / __774152 / __b5847c — two different orders, each captured as the detail rows progressively render). Fully id-less (only `dasher_workflow_host_fragment`), so anchored on the text PAIR 'Customer received order' + 'Correct order delivered'; both are corpus-unique (0 other snapshots), both are present even in the sparsest fielded render (…__7e986d, which has none of the expanded detail lines), and the pair keeps the rule off the sibling ORDER LIST screen (which repeats the '<store> · <date> • No issues' rows but none of the check labels). UNDER-MATCH RESIDUAL: an order WITH a reported issue presumably renders different check copy and would fall to UNKNOWN until fielded — the safe direction. PRIVACY: no customer PII — MERCHANT name + date + fixed platform check labels, all driver-owned. Deliberately NOT extended to the sibling ON-TIME per-order drill-down (…UNKNOWN__2134b3), which renders the timeline as 'Pickup at <store>' / 'Delivery to <customer first name>' and therefore DOES carry customer PII; that surface stays UNKNOWN, and #962 added 'Delivery to ' to CustomerTextMarkers so its UNKNOWN envelope is scrubbed.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.order_feedback_detail",
      "priority": 138,
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "Customer received order"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "Correct order delivered"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "comment": "#962 — the 'Pause offers from stores' informational sheet reached from the rewards board's pause-offers row (a title, the explainer sentence naming the per-tier store allowance, and a Got it button). RECOGNIZE-ONLY (dev ruling 2026-07-30, #962): no state.flow, no parse. ONE fielded frame on 07-30 (…UNKNOWN__b23989), kept despite the single sighting because the issue names this surface in scope and its copy is a fixed program string rather than a transient render. Anchored on the explainer sentence's stable head 'Pause offers from specific stores for up to two weeks' — corpus-unique (0 other snapshots) and long enough to be self-anchoring; the bare title alone is NOT used, because 'Pause offers from 1 store' / 'Pause offers from up to 3 stores' rows also appear on the rewards board one rule above. DELIBERATELY NOT GENERALISED to the sibling reward-explainer sheets ('Silver offer boost', 'Priority for high paying offers' — …UNKNOWN__d30dd8 / __797dd6 / __8b658c): those render in DoorDash's generic Prism bottom-sheet chrome (`prism_sheet_title`/`prism_sheet_message`/`prism_sheet_header_image`), which is shared by sheets all over the app, and their only distinguishing text is the tier-reward NAME — enumerating those would be a per-tier vocabulary list that grows with the program, so they stay UNKNOWN. PRIVACY: no PII — fixed program copy only.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.pause_offers_info",
      "priority": 139,
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "hasTextContaining": "Pause offers from specific stores for up to two weeks"
        }
      }
    },
    {
